// Preset test inputs for the "File a Claim" form.
//
// WHAT THESE ARE: a fixed list of inputs written directly into this
// file by the developer. Nothing here is fetched at runtime or
// generated by any script. There is no autonomous claim-filing agent
// anywhere in Oraclon — every dispute that exists on this app was filed
// by a person clicking "File a Claim," whether they typed the values by
// hand or selected one of these presets first to save time. (The one
// genuine AI agent in this system is the GenVM adjudicator inside
// resolve_dispute — see docs/architecture.md — which has nothing to do
// with these presets.)
//
// WHY THEY EXIST: typing six numeric fields by hand before seeing the
// app do anything is real friction that has nothing to do with what the
// app actually demonstrates (can Oraclon correctly tell an accurate
// claim from an inaccurate one?). These presets exist only to remove
// that friction. They must never be displayed in a way that could be
// mistaken for live data or anything happening in real time — see the
// "Preset Test Inputs" table in NewDispute.jsx, which renders these as
// a plain static list, not as cards or tiles that could be confused
// with the live Agent A / Agent B claim display used elsewhere in the
// app.
//
// WHAT'S REAL AND WHAT ISN'T, PLAINLY:
//   - protocolSlug is a REAL DefiLlama protocol identifier in every
//     entry below — these are real, currently-tracked protocols, not
//     invented names. That part is factual and checkable directly at
//     https://defillama.com/protocol/<slug>.
//   - targetDate is a real past date, chosen to be safely resolvable by
//     DefiLlama's historical series for that protocol.
//   - agentAClaimedTvlE6 / agentBClaimedTvlE6 are HAND-WRITTEN TEST
//     VALUES, not live or historical fact. They are deliberately
//     constructed so that filing this exact preset exercises a specific
//     outcome (accurate/inaccurate) when Oraclon resolves it — never
//     presented as "this is protocol X's real TVL." The
//     `expectedOutcome` field documents what each preset is meant to
//     demonstrate.
//   - Only the one entry marked `liveConfirmed: true` (Aave) has
//     actually been run through live GenVM consensus and observed to
//     produce that split, twice, on real deployments. Every other entry
//     is an honestly-labeled test input that has not yet been run live —
//     the UI states this plainly rather than implying otherwise.
//
// Whenever a new entry is added, keep protocolSlug real and checkable,
// and never mark `liveConfirmed: true` until it has actually been
// resolved on a real GenVM deployment and observed to behave as
// described.

